A||||11||William Federle||Recurring starter problems||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||09-18-2000||10:53 PM||||Today was not a good fiero day. I was on my way home from work and I had the cruise on. I pressed down on the accelerator to pass someone and nothing happened. I tapped the brakes and coasted over to the side of the road and opened the trunk. The throttle linkage had come off at one end. I snapped it back on, and started down the road again.<BR>The same thing happened about 10 miles further. I snapped it back on again and stopped at the Autozone in Menominee Falls to see if they had any of those clips that are on either end of the throttle linkage. They didn't.<P>    When I got back in the car to start it, nothing. This had happened before and after about 1/2 hr, it had started - but not today.<BR>I finally gave up and called AAA. A lady tow truck driver finally showed up after an hour wait. I told her the problem as she prepared to hook up the car. She got out a big hook and crawled under the car and said to try to start it. When I did, she hit the starter with the hook and the darn thing started. I replaced this starter last winter, and it's on its second solenoid. When I installed the 2nd solenoid, I also installed a solenoid heat shield. I don't think they sell one for the starter itself. Is there any way to make these things last a year or more?<P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 09-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-18-2000||11:48 PM||||find another supply of starters. the ones you are getting stink.<P>There is a factory shield for the starter from GM. You can make one easy enough. Any stiff AL or steel sheet can be used. Just make sure it doesn't hit any wires on sharp edges. Remember this is a heat reflector not insulation. Thicknes of material doesn't matter as long as it will hold it's shape and stay in place.||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||09-19-2000||11:45 PM||||I agree with ogre but I would not use sheet steel. It will just rust and get nasty I would use stainless. It is harder to work but?||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||09-20-2000||07:03 AM||||Thanks ogre and tom. I took your advice ogre.<BR>I got a reman'd ac delco starter from a local<BR>gm dealer last night and installed it. I also ordered the heat shield from them - it will get here Friday. I hope using the starter in the meantime without it won't damage it. I just couldn't wait to get it on the car - the other one was just too unreliable.<P>The throttle linkage rod is no longer available from gm. I guess I'll have to try to find it online. The guy was able to give me a gm number for it though.<P>10030653<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ROB MAC||09-20-2000||04:15 PM||||Now about that lady that crawled under your car.......tell me more....and don't skip any details.||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||09-20-2000||08:52 PM||||Well, she wasn't exactly a miss America, but after she helped me get the car started, I'd have voted for her. She also saved me a lot of trouble-shooting. Also, she fit under a Fiero (that was sitting on all four tires) far enough to reach the starter if that tells you anything.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 09-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||ROB MAC||09-21-2000||03:48 PM||||Say, for her to do that she'd have to be kinda fl... at second thought, never mind.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gokart||09-21-2000||09:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TOM:<BR><B>I agree with ogre but I would not use sheet steel. It will just rust and get nasty I would use stainless. It is harder to work but?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>How about a piece of lead? It's pretty thick and easy to work with into place.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Gokart_sig_1.gif"> <P>Marc                     87GT||||reg||5
Z||000008||William Federle||09-24-2000||08:27 AM||||When I got the new (reman'd) starter in I had to add two shims to the three that were originally with the old starter to get it to the point that the pinion would freely engage and disengage. <P>The only thing is that the new starter  sounds a lot different than the old one did when I put that one in. It cranked fast and smooth with the old one, but with this new one, it sounds like it's pulling really hard and it doesn't start as quickly.<P>I guess there could be other things going on - but I don't think anything bad is happening in the engine - it still is getting about 32 MPG so I don't think anything is tightening up. It's getting close to time for new plugs, cap, rotor, wires, etc. Also the battery fluid was below the top of the plates when I checked it last a couple of days ago but it will start with the lights on - it's about 12.6V with no load. It goes up to 14.5 with the engine on.<P>I guess I could have gotten a bad reman'd starter from GM. Any ideas?<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||09-24-2000||02:03 PM||||If the battery is loosing "water" then it's likely bad. you need to check the whole charging system and replace the battery. Most testing machines can also tell you what the starter draws durring crank.<P>What is the part # for that heat shield? How much was it?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cooter||09-24-2000||02:27 PM||||The starter on my 3.4 had a heat shield over the solenoid. When I put the motor in my Fiero, I just used that shield on the Fiero starter. I had to slot the holes a little because the Camaro solenoid is a little smaller, but it fit OK. Maybe a junkyard core or rebuilder would have one for free.||||reg||1
Z||000011||William Federle||09-24-2000||08:22 PM||||The heat-shield is a molded rubber (feels like neoprene) covered with aluminum foil. I think it goes over both the starter and the solenoid. I will have to bet a big metal strap to hold it onto the starter and solenoid. The GM # is 10035978 and it cost $8.46 .<P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
